From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bootutil: scramble the scratch trailer instead of a no-op
 erase, and fix a backwards-scramble off-by-one (#1066)

A completed scratch-based swap can leave the scratch area's temporary
trailer magic GOOD forever, so the device resumes the same finished swap
on every subsequent boot. Two independent mechanisms in bootutil, not
one:

(i) swap_scratch.c's `!bs->use_scratch` branch (boot_swap_sectors(),
    STATE_0) writes a full temporary trailer to the scratch area --
    magic included, via swap_status_init() -- as a place to stash the
    swap-type while the primary slot's own trailer is being rebuilt.
    The comment right after it, "Erase the temporary trailer from the
    scratch area", is meant to remove that magic once it is no longer
    needed. On a flash device that does not require an explicit erase
    before rewrite, that call -- a plain boot_erase_region() -- is a
    documented no-op: boot_erase_region() (bootutil_area.c) takes its
    `device_requires_erase(fa) == false` branch, logs "device without
    erase", and returns success without touching flash. The GOOD magic
    survives. On the next boot, boot_status_tables[]'s scratch-source
    entry matches on scratch magic GOOD alone, regardless of the
    primary slot's own image_ok, and swap_read_status() resumes the
    same completed swap again.

    Fixed by replacing that no-op erase with
    swap_scramble_trailer_sectors(state, fap_scratch) -- the same
    helper already used a few lines up to clear the primary slot's own
    trailer, so the trailer-scoped offset math (boot_trailer_scramble_
    offset()) is not duplicated. Scoped to the trailer, not the whole
    scratch area: nothing outside the trailer has been written to
    scratch at this point in STATE_0, so scrambling the rest would cost
    flash endurance (an MRAM write-cycle budget) for no benefit. On a
    device that DOES require an explicit erase, boot_scramble_region()'s
    own device_requires_erase() branch routes this to boot_erase_region()
    scoped to the trailer's own offset/size and backwards -- narrower and
    a different direction than the plain forward, whole-scratch-area
    erase this replaces, not identical to it. That narrowing is safe: the
    unconditional first scratch erase a few lines earlier in this same
    STATE_0 block already covers everything below the trailer, so this
    change does not leave any byte of the scratch area unerased on that
    class of device either.

    The FIRST scratch erase a few lines earlier in the same STATE_0
    block ("erasing scratch area", unconditional every sector) is
    deliberately left as a plain erase. At that point nothing has been
    written to scratch yet in this call, and both of what follows --
    swap_status_init() a few lines down, and the boot_copy_region()
    image-data copy later in the same block -- unconditionally
    overwrite in place whatever they need. A no-op there costs nothing
    beyond one leftover write block neither of them touches, and
    unlike the second erase there is no stale magic for a no-op to
    strand.

(ii) boot_scramble_region()'s backwards branch (bootutil_area.c, the
    no-erase overwrite path) never writes the block at its own
    end_offset. end_offset is computed as the range's first (lowest)
    write block, INCLUSIVE -- unlike the forward branch, where
    end_offset is one block past the last, exclusive -- but a single
    `while (off != end_offset)` entry guard shared across both
    directions treats the two identically: once off has decremented
    down to end_offset the guard is already false and the loop body
    never runs for it. For a trailer-scoped call (as (i) above now
    makes on the scratch area) that dropped block is the trailer's
    first status entry, which is exactly what would let a stale
    non-0x00 status byte survive a scramble meant to remove it.

    Fixed by splitting the shared loop into its own per-direction
    form: the backwards case now writes first and checks the boundary
    after, mirroring boot_erase_region()'s own post-op break for this
    same backwards boundary a few lines above -- that function never
    had this bug, because its `while (true)` always runs the erase
    before testing for the boundary. The forward branch is untouched,
    byte for byte.

Not bench-verified on AEN silicon, and cannot be from this tree today:
#1100 and #1445 removed the AEN family's scratch/slot1 partitions to
fit the disjoint dual-core slot0 budget, so no AEN board target
currently builds the scratch-swap path either of these touches. This
closes the mechanism ahead of a secondary slot being reintroduced.

Does NOT close #1066's second finding (a clean, valid PERM upgrade
request that produces no swap at all). That trace runs through a
stale non-0x00 primary status byte surviving into the next boot
(boot_status_is_reset() false) rather than the scratch mechanism this
patches, and fix (ii) plausibly removes its source -- but that link is
a hypothesis, not proven here, and is not claimed as closed.

Regression-verified on the mcuboot host simulator, companion
0005-sim-scratch-trailer-no-erase-device-regression-test.patch.

Signed-off-by: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
---
diff --git a/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_area.c b/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_area.c
index 3bd6bb0..34fc4b8 100644
--- a/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_area.c
+++ b/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_area.c
@@ -326,26 +326,63 @@ boot_scramble_region(const struct flash_area *fa, uint32_t off, uint32_t size, b
         BOOT_LOG_DBG("boot_scramble_region: start offset %" PRIu32 ", "
                      "end offset %" PRIu32, off, end_offset);
 
-        while (off != end_offset) {
-            /* Write over the area to scramble data that is there */
-            rc = flash_area_write(fa, off, buf, write_block);
-            if (rc != 0) {
-                BOOT_LOG_DBG("boot_scramble_region: error %d for %p "
-                             "%" PRIu32 " %u",
-                             rc, fa, off, (unsigned int)write_block);
-                break;
-            }
+        if (backwards) {
+            /* (alp-sdk#1066) end_offset here is the FIRST (lowest) write
+             * block of the range, INCLUSIVE -- unlike the forward branch
+             * below, where end_offset is one block past the LAST block,
+             * exclusive. A single `while (off != end_offset)` entry guard
+             * shared across both directions treated the two the same way,
+             * so it never wrote the block at end_offset: by the time off
+             * had decremented down to it, the guard was already false and
+             * the loop body never ran for it. For a trailer-scoped call
+             * that dropped block is the trailer's first status entry,
+             * letting a stale non-0x00 status byte survive a scramble
+             * that was supposed to remove it.
+             *
+             * Fixed by writing first and checking the boundary after,
+             * mirroring boot_erase_region()'s own post-op break for this
+             * same backwards boundary a few lines above -- that function
+             * never had this bug because its `while (true)` always runs
+             * the body before testing for the boundary. The comparison
+             * stays `>=`, not `==`: off and end_offset are both write-block
+             * aligned for every caller today so the two are equivalent on
+             * reachable shapes, but `>=` cannot walk below the requested
+             * range if a future caller ever passes an off/size whose sum
+             * is not write_block-aligned, where `==` would skip past
+             * end_offset and keep decrementing.
+             */
+            while (true) {
+                /* Write over the area to scramble data that is there */
+                rc = flash_area_write(fa, off, buf, write_block);
+                if (rc != 0) {
+                    BOOT_LOG_DBG("boot_scramble_region: error %d for %p "
+                                 "%" PRIu32 " %u",
+                                 rc, fa, off, (unsigned int)write_block);
+                    break;
+                }
 
-            MCUBOOT_WATCHDOG_FEED();
+                MCUBOOT_WATCHDOG_FEED();
 
-            if (backwards) {
-                if (end_offset >= off) {
-                    /* Reached the first offset in range and already scrambled it */
+                if (end_offset >= off) {
+                    /* Reached the first offset in range and already scrambled it */
                     break;
                 }
 
                 off -= write_block;
-            } else {
+            }
+        } else {
+            while (off != end_offset) {
+                /* Write over the area to scramble data that is there */
+                rc = flash_area_write(fa, off, buf, write_block);
+                if (rc != 0) {
+                    BOOT_LOG_DBG("boot_scramble_region: error %d for %p "
+                                 "%" PRIu32 " %u",
+                                 rc, fa, off, (unsigned int)write_block);
+                    break;
+                }
+
+                MCUBOOT_WATCHDOG_FEED();
+
                 off += write_block;
 
                 if (end_offset <= off) {
diff --git a/boot/bootutil/src/swap_scratch.c b/boot/bootutil/src/swap_scratch.c
index 59b64ad..09f34f6 100644
--- a/boot/bootutil/src/swap_scratch.c
+++ b/boot/bootutil/src/swap_scratch.c
@@ -746,6 +746,18 @@ boot_swap_sectors(int idx, uint32_t sz, struct boot_loader_state *state,
     bs->use_scratch = (bs->idx == BOOT_STATUS_IDX_0 && copy_sz != sz);
 
     if (bs->state == BOOT_STATUS_STATE_0) {
+        /* (alp-sdk#1066) Plain erase, deliberately left as-is on a device
+         * that does not require an explicit erase before rewrite: nothing
+         * has been written to the scratch area yet at this point, and
+         * both of what follows in this STATE_0 block -- swap_status_init()
+         * a few lines down, and the boot_copy_region() image-data copy
+         * below -- unconditionally overwrite in place whatever they need,
+         * so a no-op erase here costs nothing beyond the one leftover
+         * write block neither of them touches (harmless: nothing reads it
+         * before it is itself overwritten by the same code path on the
+         * next sector). Unlike the erase a few lines down, there is no
+         * stale magic here for a no-op to strand.
+         */
         BOOT_LOG_DBG("erasing scratch area");
         rc = boot_erase_region(fap_scratch, 0, flash_area_get_size(fap_scratch), false);
         assert(rc == 0);
@@ -769,9 +781,38 @@ boot_swap_sectors(int idx, uint32_t sz, struct boot_loader_state *state,
                 rc = swap_status_init(state, fap_primary_slot, bs);
                 assert(rc == 0);
 
-                /* Erase the temporary trailer from the scratch area. */
-                rc = boot_erase_region(fap_scratch, 0,
-                        flash_area_get_size(fap_scratch), false);
+                /* (alp-sdk#1066) This one DOES strand a stale magic if
+                 * left as a plain erase: on a device that does not
+                 * require an explicit erase before rewrite,
+                 * boot_erase_region() is a documented no-op here (it logs
+                 * "device without erase" and returns success without
+                 * touching flash -- see boot_erase_region()'s own
+                 * device_requires_erase() branch), so the GOOD magic
+                 * swap_status_init() just wrote a few lines up survives
+                 * in the scratch area forever. The next boot then matches
+                 * boot_status_tables[]'s scratch-source entry (magic
+                 * GOOD, primary ANY) regardless of image_ok, and resumes
+                 * this same completed swap indefinitely.
+                 *
+                 * Scramble instead of erase, and scope it to the trailer
+                 * via swap_scramble_trailer_sectors() (already used a few
+                 * lines up for the primary slot's own trailer) rather
+                 * than the whole scratch area: nothing outside the
+                 * trailer has been written to scratch at this point, so
+                 * scrambling the rest would cost flash endurance for no
+                 * benefit. On a device that DOES require an explicit
+                 * erase, boot_scramble_region()'s own
+                 * device_requires_erase() branch routes this to
+                 * boot_erase_region() scoped to the trailer's own
+                 * offset/size and backwards -- narrower, and a
+                 * different direction, than the plain forward,
+                 * whole-scratch-area erase this replaces, not
+                 * identical to it. That narrowing is safe: the
+                 * unconditional first scratch erase a few lines above
+                 * ("erasing scratch area") already covers everything
+                 * below the trailer on that class of device too.
+                 */
+                rc = swap_scramble_trailer_sectors(state, fap_scratch);
                 assert(rc == 0);
             }
         }
